## Keating: TUI, pi framework, Generative UI tools, and E2E testing

### 1. The TUI implementation

Keating has **two separate terminal UIs**, both launched from `src/cli/main.ts` (dispatch at lines ~501–513):

- **`keating shell` → the classic Pi TUI** (`launchShell`). This is the real, full-featured interface. It runs the hyperteacher extension in-process on top of `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` + `@earendil-works/pi-tui`. Interactive tool cards and `ctx.ui.custom` forms work here.
- **`keating tui` → the OpenTUI host** (`src/tui/opentui-host.ts`, `launchOpenTui`). Built on `@opentui/core` (`BoxRenderable`, `InputRenderable`, `ScrollBoxRenderable`, `TextRenderable`, `createCliRenderer`). It is a **thin RPC client** that talks to Pi over RPC (`launchRpcClient` from `src/runtime/pi.ts`).

State of the OpenTUI host: **partial / text-only**. It renders a header, a status line, a scrollable transcript, and an input box. It streams assistant `message_update`/`message_end` events as plain text. Critically, it does **not** render any interactive/generative UI. When an extension issues an `extension_ui_request` with method `select`/`confirm`/`input`/`editor`, it posts a notice ("This extension requested a Pi-specific input surface. Switch with /shell to use it.") and **cancels the request** (`sendUiResponse(id, { cancelled: true })`, opentui-host.ts lines 176–179). Typing `/shell` tears down OpenTUI and hands off to the classic Pi shell.

TUI-side rendering code (used by the classic Pi shell, not OpenTUI):
- `src/pi/hyper-teacher/tui-components.ts` — `renderCard`, `renderArtifactPreview`, `renderQuizCard`, `renderGoalCard`, `renderGoalListCard`, and the interactive `AnswerFormComponent` (keyboard-driven quiz form built from pi-tui `Input`/`SelectList`).
- `src/pi/hyper-teacher/header-component.ts`, `setup-wizard.ts`, `ui-helpers.ts`.

### 2. Generative UI tools — web vs TUI support

Two parallel tool implementations exist:
- **CLI/TUI extension tools:** `src/pi/hyper-teacher/tools/*` registered via `registerKeatingTools` (`tools/index.ts`), built with `keatingToolMaker` in `tools/shared.ts`. Each tool can attach a `renderResult(result, options, theme)` that returns a pi-tui `Component`.
- **Web browser tools:** `web/src/keating/browser-tools/*` (`assessment.ts`, `teaching.ts`, `media.ts`, `improvement.ts`, `workspace.ts`), built with `createTool` (`browser-tools/shared.ts`), returning **strings that embed interactive tags**.

**Web rendering mechanism (per project memory, confirmed):** tools emit a self-closing custom tag with a **double-JSON-encoded** payload: `` `&lt;keating-quiz json=${JSON.stringify(JSON.stringify(quiz))} /&gt;` `` (e.g. `assessment.ts:177`, `teaching.ts:76/287/338`, `media.ts:298/422`, `AssistantChatPanel.tsx:3569`). `web/src/components/AssistantChatPanel.tsx` parses these with regexes (`quizTagPattern`, `sceneTagPattern`, `questionTagPattern`, `goalTagPattern`, `generatedImageTagPattern`, `quizResultTagPattern`, ~lines 1255–1260) and renders React components (`QuizRenderer`, `QuestionRenderer`, `GoalRenderer`, `QuizResultCard`, `FlashcardRenderer`, `MermaidRenderer`, `SceneRenderer`). User interaction dispatches **window CustomEvents** (`keating:quiz-submitted`, `keating:question-answered`, `keating:card-reviewed`, `keating:quiz-remediation-requested`, `keating:quiz-reframe-requested`), which listeners feed back into <truncated omitted_approx_tokens="1045" />`custom`, `notify`, `setStatus`) and stores the active context via `setActiveCtx` (shared.ts). The web app does not load the pi extension; it re-implements the tools as browser tools and drives the agent via `useKeatingAgent` (`web/src/hooks/useKeatingAgent.tsx`) using `@earendil-works/pi-agent-core` `Agent`, `@earendil-works/pi-ai`, and `defaultConvertToLlm` from `@earendil-works/pi-web-ui`.

**Gaps where the OpenTUI host lags web/classic shell:** no interactive cards, no `select`/`confirm`/`input`/`editor` surfaces (cancelled outright), no quiz/goal/question/flashcard/image/scene rendering, no `resultId` feedback loop UI — it only streams text and offers `/shell` as an escape hatch.

### 4. Testing harness and API keys

- Tests run with **`bun test`**: root suite `bun test ./test/*.test.ts` (justfile `test`), web suite `cd web &amp;&amp; bun test` (justfile `test-web`). Web has two dirs: `web/src/test/*.test.ts` (~65 files) and `web/test/*.test.ts`. Strategy doc: `docs/TESTING.md` (Antithesis-style "system laws", property/fuzz tests via `fast-check`; also Stryker mutation testing via `stryker.config.json`).
- **How e2e tests obtain API keys:** environment variables, gated with early-return guards — **not** a secret manager. The real-model tests use Gemini by default: `test/fuzz.test.ts:16`, `test/evolution.test.ts:168/189`, `test/map-elites.test.ts:279` all do `if (!process.env.GOOGLE_API_KEY &amp;&amp; !process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY) return;` (silently skip when no key). `test/package.test.ts` exercises env plumbing for `OPENAI_API_KEY`, `GEMINI_API_KEY`, `GOOGLE_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, `ZYPHRA_API_KEY`. No test hardcodes or fetches a live key.
- **Skate:** there is **no skate integration in code or tests**. The only references are `docs/TUTORIAL.md:45` ("...or use the `skate` secret manager if available") as an optional suggestion for storing keys, and `crush.json:50` where `"using-skate"` is a **disabled Crush skill** (unrelated to Keating runtime). Provider auth/env handling lives in `src/core/provider-auth.ts` (`PROVIDER_ENV_KEYS`, `envWithProviderAliases`) — env-var based.
